High schools
  科學班

1. We can get matching year data.
2. Yes I know this implied score is a threshold, that's all we got. I also know x% gpa means a different thing at different schools. It's all we got.
3. Gates are either a subtest score or a total score. It seems that the data is very thin - just the 12%, 25%, and 50% levels rounded to n/15. We can still work with that, trying to fit the percent of people achieving each score, even if it's noisy. Or you could try to go digging for better data. We also know that the test score is defined as follows:
  Take the top 1% of scores
  Average, this is the threshold for 15
  All other ranks are proportional
So we actually know about 6 points on the curve and can assume it's normal (due to each test item having a percent chance of completion) or some similarly simple distribution. Fit to this distribution and compute the number of students at each score level. Or in the absolute worst case we can simulate.

We also don't know the correlation between subtests or the count for people who took different subsets. They are likely correlated around 0.85 but we are going to effectively treat it as 1.0. This is what the ceec_score.formula_percentile does.

As a followup, we count try to simulate a correlation between high school GPA and xuece. Or we could simulate different means and sds at different high schools on xuece combining with the cutoff.

This is important because it's 50% of admits.


